  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 244 AM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nEnsemble guidance is quite confident on upper-level troughing \nThursday to start the four-day forecast period, placing us in strong \nsouthwest flow aloft through Friday morning. A decent jet streak is \nanticipated to present over our County Warning Area (CWA) on the lee \nside of the Rossby wave. Furthermore, a surface low southeast of the \nregion should continue moving east across the Central Plains. The \npattern likely changes over the course of the day Friday as the \ntrough propagates eastward, bringing us into zonal westerly flow. \nMoving forward, weak ridging starts to develop Saturday shifting 300-\n500mb flow to the northwest. Said ridge should move to the east \nfairly quickly Sunday, placing the Tri-State area right back in \nsouthwest flowing heading into next week. Other than Thursday (10-\n25%), Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) is expected to be very low \n(<5%) through next weekend. There is low chance (25%) for overnight \ntemperatures at or below freezing Friday night. Therefore, frost \nwill be possible.\n\nOverall, below average temperatures are likely as the system aloft \nushers in cool/dry air from the northwest. High temperatures upper \n50s into the low 60s will be likely Thursday and Friday. This will \nnot last as the shift to southwesterly flow kicks off a warming \ntrend that raises high temps into the mid/upper 60s Saturday as well \nas mid to upper 70s Sunday. As mentioned in the above section, low \nconfidence (20-30%) exists of temperatures falling below freezing in \nsome locations throughout the CWA Friday night into Saturday \nmorning. Low temperatures in the mid 30s will also be possible \nThursday night in some areas near or west of K-27. \n\nFire weather could be a concern late next week into the weekend, so \ntrends regarding surface winds and relative humidity (RH) will need \nto be monitored. RH is forecasted to decrease into the upper teens \nSaturday and Sunday in the eastern Colorado counties. RH values \nshould generally range around 20-40% during the day elsewhere in the \nCWA. Winds originally come on strong Thursday at 20-35 mph, gusting \n30-50 mph from the north. Fridays winds likely will be quite a bit \nweaker at 10-20 mph, also from the north, gusting up to 30 mph. \nSaturday and Sunday will be the days to keep a close eye on for \npotential fire weather. Saturday's winds will vary around the region \n15-25 mph from the south and gusting 20-35 mph. To close out the \nweekend, Sunday's winds should be quite light (5-10 mph) shifting \nfrom the southwest to southeast and gusting up to 15 mph.",
